      "sha1": "",
      "snippet": "\nWilliams, J.\nThis case presents the question of whether a trial court properly barred the press and public from a pretrial suppression hearing. We hold that it did.\nThe stipulated facts giving rise to this action against a state officer are as follows:\nOn November 1, 1978, the State of Washington charged Jo Elliott Tharp with the crime of murder in the second degree, alleging that on March 30, ",
      "type": "lead-opinion"
    },
    {
      "author_id": null,
      "cites": [],
      "download_url": null,
      "id": 9578540,
      "joined_by_ids": [],
      "local_path": null,
      "meta": {
        "timestamp": "2025-02-26T05:47:07.545807Z",
        "date_created": "2023-08-21T21:46:13.136359Z"
      },
      "ordering_key": 3,
      "per_curiam": false,
      "sha1": "",
      "snippet": "\nDolliver, J.\n(dissenting) \u2014 The majority and the concurrence begin with an erroneous premise: that constitutional rights conflict. They thus reach an erroneous result: that a balance can and should be struck. As long as the court has the view that the constitutional right of the public (which includes the press, see Cohen v. Everett City Council, 85 Wn.2d 385, 535 P.2d 801 (1975)) to the open administration of ju",
      "type": "dissent"
    }
